    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ffc418a0-6451-5186-8deb-6c1e5f3547c3",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4y,$ find the points $(x,y),$ for $0\\le y<\\frac{\\pi}{4} ,$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4y) \\\\  & =2x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4y) \\\\  & =16x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=2(x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4y)\\,i+16x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4y\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$ \n\n$$\n2(x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4y)\\,i+16x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4y\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2(x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4y)=0 \\\\ 16x\\operatorname{sin}⁡4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4y.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32\\operatorname{cos}⁡4y\\operatorname{sin}⁡4y & =0 \\\\ 16\\operatorname{sin}⁡8y & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=2.$\n\n- For $y=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ we get $x=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0,$ for $0\\le y<\\frac{\\pi}{4} ,$ at two points: $(2,0)$ and $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{8}).$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "1b31fe7d-e92e-50ed-82a8-3dfa2ebdc29d",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
